---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TrapFigure from '../components/TrapFigure.astro';
import DiffusionSampler from '../components/DiffusionSampler.astro';
import Tex from '../components/Tex.astro';
import { trapFacts } from '../sim/trap-facts.js';

/** String.raw keeps TeX backslashes intact: a quoted attribute would
 *  have JS eat them, and one of them is an invalid escape. */
const tex = String.raw;

/** Every number about Fig. 1 comes from the run the page replays. */
const f = trapFacts();
const fmt = (x: number) => Math.round(x).toLocaleString('en-US');

const research = [
  {
    title: 'Inside the Trap: a renewal law for prefix-cache collapse in LLM agent serving',
    href: '/research/inside-the-trap',
    meta: 'Ali Jannesari · Computer Science · preprint, arXiv:2611.08902',
    blurb:
      'An agent re-sends its whole growing conversation every turn, and the serving engine keeps that prefix in a GPU cache so the next turn stays cheap. A burst of new sessions can tip the engine into a trap in which every turn misses and the backlog does not drain, at a load it served before. Registered tests on two engines, two GPUs and two models; a renewal law, proved for an idealization of the trapped state and checked retrospectively against 57 stuck runs; and a session cap that prevented the trap in all 30 capped runs.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Langevin dynamics: sampling, discretization bias, transport coefficients',
    meta: 'David Herzog · Mathematics · ongoing',
    blurb:
      'The sampler underneath score-based generative models and stochastic-gradient Langevin dynamics, analyzed where its errors are computable: discretization bias against exact stationary laws, and estimator design for effective diffusivity and mobility.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Large-deviation asymptotics for mean-field interacting systems',
    meta: 'Ruoyu Wu · Mathematics · ongoing',
    blurb:
      'Rate functionals and the variational characterization of rare events for the empirical measure of a mean-field particle system, currently in the setting of load balancing. This is the construction that sits one level above the mean-field limit of wide-network training, and the machinery underneath PAC-Bayes.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Stability of neural approximations to evolution equations',
    meta: 'Jue Yan · Mathematics · ongoing',
    blurb:
      'Conservation in a cell-average neural network solver is architectural. Stability is not, and the training loss does not separate the networks that survive a long rollout from the ones that come apart. Adapting von Neumann analysis to say which, from the weights alone.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Online calibration of neural volatility forecasts with early, one-sided feedback',
    meta: 'Hongyang Gao · Computer Science · ongoing',
    blurb:
      'A forecast of a cumulative quantity is scored only when its horizon ends, but a miss becomes certain the moment the running sum crosses the bound. A calibration layer that credits those certain misses early and keeps accounts of what is still pending, with a coverage guarantee written up as a proof draft and tested numerically.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reliability of tool-using agents',
    meta: 'Yang Li · Computer Science · ongoing',
    blurb:
      'When an agent stalls, when is restarting it worth more than waiting, and how do you estimate the probability of a failure too rare for direct simulation to reach? Two planned pilots on real agents, with the direction still being chosen.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Akshat Valse · applied probability for the systems that run machine learning"
  description="Undergraduate researcher at Iowa State University in applied probability for the systems that run machine learning. Inside the Trap (arXiv:2611.08902): why a burst of agent traffic can trap a prefix-cached LLM serving engine, a renewal law for the trapped state, and a session cap that gets the engine out."
>
  <TrapFigure slot="hero" />

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail">
        <span class="rail-label">Fig. 1</span>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <dl>
          <dt>Engine</dt>
          <dd>the paper's simulator: SGLang, A100, 300k-token KV pool</dd>
          <dt>Load</dt>
          <dd>{f.mult} λ<sub>ref</sub>, plus {f.burstN} sessions at {fmt(f.tB)} s</dd>
          <dt>Cap</dt>
          <dd>{f.cap} sessions, {fmt(f.engage)}–{fmt(f.release)} s (lower lane)</dd>
          <dt>Checked</dt>
          <dd>served prompt {fmt(f.trapped.meanPrompt)} vs model F {fmt(f.model.Pz)}</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>0</span><span class="legend-ramp"></span><span>1</span>
        </div>
        <p class="legend-note">Color: cache hit fraction</p>
        <p class="legend-note">Height: sessions, √ scale</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="lede">
          I’m an undergraduate researcher at Iowa State University, working in applied
          probability on the systems that run machine learning. My preprint with Ali
          Jannesari, <a href="/research/inside-the-trap"><em>Inside the Trap</em></a>,
          explains why a burst of agent traffic can leave an LLM serving engine stuck at
          a load it served before, and what gets it out.
        </p>

        <p>
          An agent works in a loop: model call, tool call, model call, re-sending the
          whole growing conversation each turn. Serving engines such as SGLang and vLLM
          keep that shared prefix in a GPU KV cache, so a follow-up turn whose prefix is
          still cached prefills only its new tokens; in the paper’s workload a hit
          prefills 551 tokens and a miss about 11,100. The cached KV belongs to sessions
          that sit idle in tool calls, and admitting waiting work evicts it. A burst of
          new sessions can therefore tip the engine into a trap in which every turn
          misses and the backlog does not drain. In registered paired tests on Iowa
          State’s Nova cluster, on two engines, two GPUs and two models, a 200-session
          burst left the engine at zero hits to the end in all 30 runs (the registered
          rule was met at 9 of 10 rates), every matched no-burst run stayed good, and a
          client-side session cap prevented the trap in all 30 capped runs.
        </p>

        <p>
          The paper then asks what the trapped state is. It idealizes that state as a
          fluid FIFO server on which every turn misses, model F, and proves that along
          FIFO orbits its cycles obey a linear renewal recursion. The trap drains if and
          only if arrivals are below the all-miss capacity
          <Tex src={tex`\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />; above it, a Perron root carries
          every trapped state to a known self-similar regime in which the turns served
          are young and short. For the workload studied, sessions then complete faster
          than <Tex src={tex`\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />, so in the model a trap’s
          measured throughput overstates the load at which it drains. With nothing
          fitted to trap data, the theory matches 57 stuck runs retrospectively, and it
          was also tested blind in outcome. Of two ways out, refusing arrivals sheds
          sessions, while a session cap restores cache reuse: in registered tests it
          rescued 9 of 9 established traps at the same load without shedding a session.
          The paper does not prove that the engine converges to the model.
        </p>

        <p>
          The band above runs the paper’s simulator in your browser from a fixed seed:
          the engine calibrated to SGLang on an A100 with a 300,000-token KV pool,
          iteration by iteration, the same code that the site’s checks run against the
          paper’s Python engine, count for count. Both lanes get the same agent traffic:
          a Poisson stream of {fmt(f.baseSessions)} sessions at {f.mult} times the
          measured all-miss rate, plus a burst of {f.burstN} at {fmt(f.tB)} s, and both
          lose their cache hits by {fmt(f.hitsGone[0])} s. The top engine is left alone
          and stays at zero hits to the end, with about {fmt(f.trapped.sessions)} sessions
          backed up over the last 3,000 s; it is still trapped after the last burst
          session finishes, at {fmt(f.lastBurst)} s. The bottom one gets a cap of
          {f.cap} sessions at {fmt(f.engage)} s: hits come back by {fmt(f.hitsBack)} s,
          drop out again while the gate drains the backlog, and hold from
          {fmt(f.hitsHold)} s; the cap comes off at {fmt(f.release)} s, and the engine
          stays good. Over the last 3,000 s the trapped engine serves turns that average
          {fmt(f.trapped.meanPrompt)} prompt tokens, against {fmt(f.model.Pz)} in model F
          and {fmt(f.model.Pstat)} in the normal turn mix, and completes sessions at
          {f.trapped.completionsOverMiss.toFixed(2)} times
          <Tex src={tex`\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} /> against model F’s
          {f.model.DoverMiss.toFixed(2)}: young, short turns, as the theorem says, with
          nothing fitted.
        </p>

        <p class="note">
          This run was chosen from {f.selection.candidates} seeds: of the
          {f.selection.cleared} in which every burst session finishes before the end,
          the {f.selection.inRange} whose cap comes off inside the paper’s engine range
          ({fmt(f.selection.releaseRange[0])}–{fmt(f.selection.releaseRange[1])} s),
          and of those the one whose completion rate is closest to model F’s. Over
          {f.further.seeds} further seeds that played no part in the choice, every
          engine that gets the burst and no cap is stuck at zero hits
          ({f.further.stuck} of {f.further.seeds}); in the {f.further.cleared} where the
          burst sessions all finish, {f.further.clearedNoHitsAfter} stay trapped after the
          last one; and the trapped engines’ served prompts average
          {f.further.promptOverPz.toFixed(2)} times model F’s, while their completion
          rates average {f.further.completionsOverMiss.toFixed(2)} times
          <Tex src={tex`\lambda_{\mathrm{miss}}`} />, from
          {f.further.completionsMin.toFixed(2)} to {f.further.completionsMax.toFixed(2)}.
        </p>

        <p class="more">
          <a href="/research/inside-the-trap">The paper: summary, abstract, code and citation →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail">
        <span class="rail-label">Fig. 2</span>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <dl>
          <dt>Object</dt>
          <dd>VP-SDE, reverse time</dd>
          <dt>Score</dt>
          <dd>exact, not learned</dd>
          <dt>Steps</dt>
          <dd>320 Euler–Maruyama</dd>
          <dt>Graded on</dt>
          <dd>mode coverage</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">The toolkit underneath</h2>
        <p>
          The trap paper is a renewal argument about a queue, and the rest of my work is
          where that kind of argument comes from: Langevin dynamics and what its
          discretization costs, large deviations and probabilities too small for direct
          simulation to reach, and whether a PDE solver with a network inside it holds
          together when iterated. The habit is the same throughout: a closed form where
          one exists and a registered prediction where one does not, and a check that
          runs either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Generative models are hard to evaluate because the target is unknown, so a
          sampler can only be judged on whether its output looks plausible. Make the
          target a Gaussian mixture and that changes. The mixture is closed under the
          variance-preserving forward kernel, so every marginal <Tex src={tex`p_t`} /> is
          again a mixture and the score <Tex src={tex`\nabla\log p_t`} /> is available in
          closed form. Anderson's time reversal
          <Tex src={tex`dX = \big[-\tfrac{1}{2}\beta(t)X - \beta(t)\nabla\log p_t(X)\big]dt + \sqrt{\beta(t)}\,d\bar{W}`} block />
          then runs with an oracle score, so whatever error remains belongs to the
          sampler and not to a network. The weights below are deliberately unequal,
          which is what makes mode collapse detectable at all. A sampler that drops the
          0.08 component still emits individually plausible points, and only the
          proportions give it away.
        </p>

        <DiffusionSampler />

        <p>
          Over 16,384 samples the recovered weights match to
          <Tex src={tex`\max_k|\hat{w}_k - w_k| = 0.006`} /> against a Monte Carlo
          standard error of <Tex src={tex`0.0035`} />, the component means to
          <Tex src={tex`0.010`} />, and the within-component spread to
          <Tex src={tex`0.262`} /> against <Tex src={tex`\sigma = 0.26`} />. This is what
          a toy target with a closed form buys you. The sampler either returns the
          weights or it does not, and no quantity of plausible-looking output makes up
          the difference.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail"><span class="rail-label">research</span></div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Research</h2>
        {research.map((r) => (
          <article class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">{r.href ? <a href={r.href}>{r.title}</a> : r.title}</h3>
            <p class="entry-meta">{r.meta}</p>
            <p>{r.blurb}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
        <p class="more"><a href="/research">Full descriptions →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail"><span class="rail-label">elsewhere</span></div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Elsewhere</h2>
        <dl class="links">
          <dt>Preprint</dt>
          <dd><a href="/research/inside-the-trap">Inside the Trap</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08902">arXiv:2611.08902</a>)</dd>
          <dt>Code</dt>
          <dd><a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse/surviving-llm-stampede">surviving-llm-stampede</a> · <a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse/langevin-samplers">langevin-samplers</a></dd>
          <dt>Email</dt>
          <dd><a href="mailto:avalse@iastate.edu">avalse@iastate.edu</a></dd>
          <dt>GitHub</dt>
          <dd><a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse" rel="me">AkshatValse</a></dd>
          <dt>CV</dt>
          <dd><a href="/cv">/cv</a></dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="pending">Further preprints will be posted here as they become available.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .more { margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  .pending {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono), monospace;
  }
